= Contact-Consistent Grasp Planning <sec:planning>

== Architecture

Palisade is organised as three concurrent loops running at different rates,
shown in @fig:architecture. The vision loop runs once per grasp attempt and
produces a set of candidate approach poses from a single depth image. The
tactile loop runs at 180 Hz and maintains the contact state described in
@sec:patch. The revision loop runs at 60 Hz during closure, rescores the
surviving candidates against the current contact state, and either confirms the
active candidate, commands a lateral correction, or requests a fresh approach.

The separation is deliberate. Rescoring every candidate at the tactile rate
would be wasteful, since the contact state changes slowly relative to the taxel
scan, and it would couple the planner to sensor noise. Rescoring only once, at
first contact, would miss the redistribution that occurs as normal force
builds, which is exactly the phase in which a deformable object reveals its
behaviour.

