== Candidate generation

Candidates are generated by the standard antipodal procedure. The depth image
is converted to a point cloud, surface normals are estimated over a 6 mm
neighbourhood, and pairs of points whose normals are within 22 degrees of
antiparallel and whose separation lies inside the gripper stroke are retained.
Each pair defines a grasp axis, and a rotation about that axis is sampled at 15
degree increments. Kinematic reachability and a collision check against the
scene mesh prune the set. A typical bin scene yields between 300 and 1800
surviving candidates. This part of the pipeline follows established practice
@tenpas2017grasp and is not a contribution of this thesis.

== A patch-conditioned quality metric <sec:metric>

The classical measure of @eq:qfc assumes a point contact. We replace it with a
measure over the observed patch. The construction proceeds in three steps.

*Step one: discretise the patch.* Partition $Omega$ into $q$ cells of roughly
equal normal force, using a k-means split on the taxel coordinates weighted by
pressure. Each cell $k$ contributes a sub-contact at its own centroid $x_k$
with normal force $F_k$ and local surface normal $n_k$ recovered from the
gradient of the pressure field. In practice $q = 5$ is sufficient: raising it
to 9 changes the resulting quality by less than 1.5 percent while tripling the
hull computation time.

*Step two: aperture per cell.* The friction cone aperture of cell $k$ is not
the global $hat(mu)$ but a value reduced by the fraction of the cell already in
partial slip. Let $sigma_k in [0, 1]$ be the stuck fraction of cell $k$,
estimated from the shear field as described in @sec:slip. We set

$ mu_k = hat(mu) sigma_k^(1 / 2). $ <eq:mu-cell>

The square root is empirical. A linear reduction proved too aggressive, in the
sense that it drove the planner to reject grasps that in fact held, and no
reduction at all left the metric blind to the boundary annulus.

*Step three: hull and radius.* Each cell contributes $m = 8$ generator wrenches
built from its own cone. The patch-conditioned quality is

$ Q_"pc" = min_(w in partial "conv"(cal(W)_"pc")) norm(w), quad
  cal(W)_"pc" = union.big_(k=1)^q { vec(g_(k,j), (x_k - p) times g_(k,j)) }_(j=1)^m. $ <eq:qpc>

#proposition("reduction to the classical measure")[
  If the patch degenerates to a single cell at $x_1$ with $sigma_1 = 1$, then
  $cal(W)_"pc"$ coincides with the primitive wrench set of @eq:wrenchset and
  $Q_"pc" = Q_"FC"$.
]

The reduction matters because it makes the comparison with a point-contact
planner a comparison of contact models rather than of metrics. Any difference
in behaviour must come from the patch, not from a different notion of quality.

== Deformation penalty

A grasp that is excellent in wrench space may still be unacceptable because it
crushes the object. We add a penalty term driven by the observed compliance.
During closure the estimator records the relation between commanded finger
displacement $delta$ and measured total normal force $F$. For a locally linear
material this relation has slope $k_"obj"$, and the penalty

$ Pi(F) = cases(
  0 & "if" F <= F_"safe",
  rho (F - F_"safe")^2 & "otherwise"
), quad F_"safe" = F_"req" + 2 sqrt(k_"obj") / rho, $ <eq:penalty>

discourages force beyond what the task requires. $F_"req"$ is the normal force
needed to resist the transport wrench at the current $hat(mu)$, and $rho$ is
set so that the penalty equals the typical spread of $Q_"pc"$ at twice
$F_"safe"$. The planner maximises $Q_"pc" - Pi(F)$.

@fig:cone shows the geometric consequence of the patch conditioning. The single
wide cone that a point-contact planner assumes is replaced by five narrower
cones whose apertures differ, and the boundary cells, whose stuck fraction has
already fallen, contribute least.

