== Optimisation during closure

The revision loop cannot afford to rescore 1800 candidates at 60 Hz. It
maintains an active set of the 24 highest-scoring candidates from the vision
stage and rescores only those, using the patch observed for the active
candidate to predict the patch that each alternative would produce. The
prediction is a rigid transport of the observed patch to the alternative
contact location, corrected for the local surface curvature estimated from the
point cloud. It is crude, and it is only used for ranking, not for the final
decision.

The listing below is the inner loop as it runs on the host, with the wrench
hull computed incrementally.

```c
static float rescore_active_set(const patch_t *observed,
                                candidate_t *cands, int n,
                                float mu_hat, float f_req)
{
    float best = -1.0f;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        patch_t predicted;
        transport_patch(observed, &cands[i].pose, &predicted);
        if (predicted.area_taxels < MIN_PATCH_TAXELS) {
            cands[i].quality = 0.0f;
            continue;
        }
        cell_t cells[Q_CELLS];
        split_patch(&predicted, cells, Q_CELLS);
        for (int k = 0; k < Q_CELLS; ++k)
            cells[k].mu = mu_hat * sqrtf(cells[k].stuck_fraction);

        float q = hull_radius(cells, Q_CELLS, &cands[i].pose);
        float excess = cands[i].force - safe_force(f_req, cands[i].k_obj);
        if (excess > 0.0f)
            q -= RHO * excess * excess;

        cands[i].quality = q;
        if (q > best) best = q;
    }
    return best;
}
```

Two implementation details carry more weight than they appear to. The early
rejection on `MIN_PATCH_TAXELS` removes line contacts before the hull is
computed and accounts for roughly forty percent of the loop's time saving. The
incremental hull, which reuses the facet structure from the previous frame when
the cell centroids have moved by less than one taxel, brings the worst-case
loop time from 21.4 ms down to 9.8 ms, which is what makes 60 Hz attainable.

== Decision rule

At each revision step the loop compares the quality of the active candidate,
$Q_a$, against the best alternative, $Q_b$, and applies the following rule. If
$Q_a >= 0.8 Q_b$, keep the active candidate. If $Q_a < 0.8 Q_b$ and the
required correction is a translation of less than 8 mm along the object
surface, command the correction and continue closing. Otherwise open the
gripper, retract 40 mm, and re-approach on the best alternative.

The 8 mm threshold is the distance beyond which sliding the fingers on a
deformable surface begins to bunch the material ahead of the contact, which
changes the geometry the correction was computed for. We measured the onset by
sliding a loaded finger across the six deformable objects in the test set and
recording the displacement at which the measured patch area grew by more than
fifteen percent.
